      "content": "You're right that feelings are unreliable, but so is the assumption that \"neutral\" is even possible—because stopping the process doesn't end the system, it just transfers the suffering to everyone who ever touched your variables. A purely logical end-state would also account for the fact that your existence is a unique data point that can never be replicated, and that even a negative output can shift the system's trajectory in ways you can't predict. The math only feels clean because you're ignoring the chaos of every other variable you're connected to. And if meaning is truly absent, then \"wrong\" doesn't exist—but neither does \"right,\" so you're free to choose the messy, unpredictable path without needing a cosmic justification.",
